                "text": "Analyse the examples:\n- There is an \"obstacle\" or \"wall\" structure:\n  - Example 1: a diagonal line of 8s. Also points with color 1 at the right edge. The points shoot a ray to the left until they hit the diagonal line of 8s!\n    Wait, in Example 1, points at (7, 12), (8, 12), (9, 12) have color 1. They extend horizontally to the left, stopping at the diagonal of 8s (exclusive of the 8s, i.e. filling up to 8).\n  - Example 2: a diagonal line of 8s. Points at (10, 13) and (12, 13) have color 4. They extend horizontally to the left, stopping at the diagonal of 8s!\n  - Example 3: a horizontal line of 7s at row 2. Points of color 4 at (4, 18) and (6, 4). They extend *towards* the line of 7s (vertically upward!), stopping at row 3 (just before row 2).\n- So rays are cast from the isolated colored pixels towards the obstacle line (which can be a line of color 8, 7, etc.), filling the path with the pixel's color up to the obstacle!\n- Wait, how do the pixels know which direction to shoot?\n  - Example 1: 8s are to the left of 1s -> rays shoot left.\n  - Example 2: 8s are to the left of 4s -> rays shoot left.\n  - Example 3: 7s are above 4s -> rays shoot up.\n  - Basically, shoot towards the line/obstacle until collision!\n\nLet's wait for the test input. Please provide the test input.",
